{"version":"1","id":"antirez","handle":"antirez","name":"antirez","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/t_logo,f_auto/v1/logos/d8d91fc3b30b407d894efa6d6c02e51f","description":null,"is_squad":false,"x":9.4004,"y":-0.6855,"cluster_id":0,"posts_collected":93,"newest_post_at":"2026-05-07T15:10:54.000Z","oldest_post_at":"2026-03-05T11:26:29.000Z","features":{"hype_score":0.005376,"listicle_ratio":0.021505,"question_ratio":0.032258,"avg_read_time":0.0,"summary_length_avg":260.804348,"title_length_avg":209.946237,"title_length_var":11264.222916,"tag_entropy":2.604629,"tag_diversity":0.182796,"top_tag_share":0.043011,"avg_upvotes":0.032258,"median_upvotes":0.0,"avg_comments":0.0,"comment_to_upvote_ratio":0.25,"zero_engagement_share":0.967742,"viral_share":0.0,"posts_per_week":10.307835,"non_article_ratio":0.989247,"author_present_share":0.0,"recency_skew":93.0},"view_coords":{"engagement_style":[-8.8382,-9.8824],"cadence_depth":[-0.7647,-9.9706],"hype_variety":[-1.1765,-5.6324],"title_style":[3.5,5.4412]},"top_tags":[["llm",6],["ai-inference",5],["ai-coding",4],["deepseek",4],["redis",3],["data-structures",3],["mac",2],["data-science",2]],"sample_titles":{"representative":["Today local inference of LLMs is a huge amount of mixed quality inference with often small details off, wrong sampling parameters, unclear real world performances (eg: qwen 3.6 with thinking is impossible bc of verbosity, but benchmarks are done this way). Not very good.","Take note about the right way to react.","Biggest mistake in AI coding era: to believe that specifications should be either natural language OR something else. The best combo is a natural language high level specification (the intend), plus code (as it gets written) documenting the finer behaviors."],"outlier":["My POV on front-end of 2026"]},"recent_posts":[{"id":"Vt4RHVvqO","title":"RT @lcamtuf: The coreutils Rust rewrite story is pretty funny.\n\nCoreutils are tools like rm, mv, mkdir, etc. Unlike binutils, this isn't a\u2026","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2051378020489851177","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/rt-lcamtuf-the-coreutils-rust-rewrite-story-is-pretty-funny-coreutils-are-tools-like-rm-mv-mkd-vt4rhvvqo","summary":"A brief social media post referencing the story of rewriting GNU coreutils (tools like rm, mv, mkdir) in Rust, described as 'pretty funny' with the thought cut off mid-sentence. There is no substantive content beyond the teaser.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--1KxV4ohY--/f_auto/v1722860400/public/Placeholder%2007","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-04T19:06:47.000Z"},{"id":"BFN2YzHF2","title":"[blog post] Redis array: short story of a long development process =>","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2051306740075901234","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/blog-post-redis-array-short-story-of-a-long-development-process--bfn2yzhf2","summary":"A development story covering the design and implementation of an array data structure in Redis, detailing the challenges and decisions made throughout a lengthy development process.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--foaA6JGU--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2004","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-04T14:23:32.000Z"},{"id":"hNq6oSP03","title":"One thing to understand about the new Array type of Redis, and the support of ARGREP, is that you can store, in Redis keys, different markdown documents (skills) that are collectively used and updated by a multitude of remote agents.","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2051298233943531764","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/one-thing-to-understand-about-the-new-array-type-of-redis-and-the-support-of-argrep-is-that-you-ca-hnq6osp03","summary":"Redis's new Array type combined with ARGREP support enables storing multiple markdown documents (skills) in Redis keys, which can be collectively accessed and updated by multiple remote agents simultaneously.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--foaA6JGU--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2004","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-04T13:49:44.000Z"},{"id":"gjydQ5nFu","title":"I just submitted a PR for a new Redis Array type :)","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2051204231902675012","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/i-just-submitted-a-pr-for-a-new-redis-array-type--gjydq5nfu","summary":"A developer has submitted a pull request to Redis proposing a new Array data type. The post is a brief personal announcement with no technical details provided about the implementation or design of the proposed type.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--0_ODbtD2--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2008","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-04T07:36:13.000Z"},{"id":"xsiVVGA2C","title":"DeepSeek v4 small KV cache + MacBook fast SSD disks = the idea that the disk is not a good target for KV cache is, in this context, totally obsolete. It works *great*. The session you see is opencode using my inference engine for DS4, saving, loading sessions from disk.","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050982689696588013","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/deepseek-v4-small-kv-cache-macbook-fast-ssd-disks-the-idea-that-the-disk-is-not-a-good-target-fo-xsivvga2c","summary":"DeepSeek v4's small KV cache combined with MacBook's fast SSD storage makes disk-based KV cache storage highly viable, challenging the conventional wisdom that disks are poor targets for KV cache. A demo shows OpenCode using a custom inference engine for DeepSeek v4, with sessions being saved and loaded from disk effectively.","image":"https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HHaNxS8WQAAbl-7.jpg","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-03T16:55:53.000Z"},{"id":"fbacolhJn","title":"dtach > tmux > screen","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050872900761071677","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/dtach-tmux-screen-fbacolhjn","summary":"A brief ranking of terminal multiplexers and session managers, placing dtach above tmux and tmux above screen. No detailed reasoning is provided \u2014 it's a pure preference statement ordering these three Unix terminal tools.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--qPvKM23u--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2009","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-03T09:39:37.000Z"},{"id":"78FFgtz9t","title":"RT @jdlichtman: Update on Erd\u0151s Problem 1196:\n\nIn joint work, we refined and adapted the proof method from GPT-5.4 Pro to give proofs of se\u2026","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050578503314076014","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/rt-jdlichtman-update-on-erd-s-problem-1196-in-joint-work-we-refined-and-adapted-the-proof-metho-78ffgtz9t","summary":"A tweet announcing progress on Erd\u0151s Problem 1196, where researchers adapted a proof method from GPT-5.4 Pro to prove several related results in joint work.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--0_ODbtD2--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2008","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-02T14:09:47.000Z"},{"id":"xenuwArNm","title":"Now linenoise supports pasting large amount of texts into the prompt, with folding.","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050526361018654980","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/now-linenoise-supports-pasting-large-amount-of-texts-into-the-prompt-with-folding--xenuwarnm","summary":"The linenoise library now supports pasting large amounts of text into the prompt, with a folding feature to handle long input gracefully.","image":"https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HHTuzJ8WcAAwWu9.jpg","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-02T10:42:36.000Z"},{"id":"e9ozaQ6rh","title":"AI coding is great for many things but wow if it sucks hard at finding good function names. It read too much code around I guess ;D","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050328843743846472","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/ai-coding-is-great-for-many-things-but-wow-if-it-sucks-hard-at-finding-good-function-names-it-read--e9ozaq6rh","summary":"A brief observation that AI coding tools struggle with generating good function names, likely because they over-index on surrounding code context.","image":"https://media.daily.dev/image/upload/s--foaA6JGU--/f_auto/v1722860399/public/Placeholder%2004","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-01T21:37:44.000Z"},{"id":"if8I0HCpg","title":"Look at this. Also opencode uses freaking 11k tokens of system prompt. Even at decent pre-fill of ~130 t/s it means waiting 84 seconds to start a session. What's the point? :D The pi agent is a lot saner here.\n\nMoreover, one could say, let's cache on disk very long common KV cache chunks, no? Hash it with all the parameters and put a sensible TTL if not used. But also: only cache it if you see it repeated N times across different sessions.","url":"https://x.com/antirez/status/2050265060866453524","comments_permalink":"https://app.daily.dev/posts/look-at-this-also-opencode-uses-freaking-11k-tokens-of-system-prompt-even-at-decent-pre-fill-of-1-if8i0hcpg","summary":"A critique of opencode's 11,000-token system prompt, which causes an ~84-second wait at session start due to prefill latency at ~130 tokens/second. The author contrasts this with a leaner 'pi agent' approach and proposes a disk-based KV cache solution: hash long common KV cache chunks with all parameters, apply a TTL, and only cache chunks seen repeated across N different sessions.","image":"https://pbs.twimg.com/media/HHP-orTWYAAvvtj.jpg","num_upvotes":0,"num_comments":0,"created_at":"2026-05-01T17:24:17.000Z"}],"similar_handles":["thekitze","thsottiaux","beyang","trikcode","thdxr"],"opposite_handles":["on_leuwcq","dailydevworld","ergq3auoe","horde","webcraft"],"similar":[{"id":"thekitze","handle":"thekitze","name":"kitze \ud83d\udee0\ufe0f tinkerer.club","is_squad":false,"x":9.224,"y":-0.7151,"cluster_id":0,"posts_coll